---
# registry has no REGISTRY_AUTH (see deployment.yaml.j2) and its Service has no auth in front of
# it either — the loopback hostPort only protects the LAN path. Any pod in {{ k8s_namespace }}
# can push or delete images over the ClusterIP, including qbittorrent's and jellyfin's
# untrusted-input neighbors. This narrows that to the pods that legitimately write here.
#
# INGRESS, as in prowlarr's flaresolverr policy and n8n's broker policy. The `isolation` check in templates/verify/ checks it on every roll rather than assuming.
#
# THREE ADMIT RULES, for three different kinds of traffic:
#
#   1. POD-NETWORK PUSHERS — image-builder's build Jobs (any image_builder_name, admitted via
#      the shared `netpol-role: image-builder` label added in
#      image-builder/templates/build-job.yaml.j2 for exactly this) and this role's own
#      selftest-push/selftest-pull Jobs. Ordinary podSelector matching; unremarkable.
#
#   2. THE PULL PATH — containerd resolves {{ k8s_registry_pull_host }} (localhost, the loopback
#      hostPort in deployment.yaml.j2) and is a HOST process, not a pod. That traffic never
#      carries a pod identity a podSelector could match, so it needs an ipBlock. See the ipBlock
#      comment below for what is and is not proven about it.
#
#   3. SOURCE-CONTROLLER — Flux's fetch of the break-glass artifact (docs/break-glass.md,
#      *GitHub, GHCR or Sigstore is unreachable*). See the DECIDED: marker on that rule.
#
# WHAT DOES NOT NEED A RULE: the readinessProbe/livenessProbe on this same port. n8n's broker
# policy left its probed port (5678) open to ALL sources specifically because kube-router's
# handling of node-sourced probe traffic under a restrictive policy was untested. It is not
# untested: flaresolverr's policy restricts its ONLY port (8191) to
# `podSelector: {app: prowlarr}` with no exception of any kind, and that pod runs 1/1 Ready
# on readiness+liveness probes over that exact port. Kubelet
# probes a pod's IP directly — not through the hostPort/loopback path — and kube-router does not
# subject that path to ingress enforcement. This policy relies on the same proven behavior rather
# than re-deriving it, and does not carve out {{ k8s_registry_port }} for probes.
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: registry
  namespace: {{ k8s_namespace }}
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: registry
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
  ingress:
    # Pod-network pushers: image-builder's build Jobs and this role's own selftest Jobs.
    - from:
        - podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              netpol-role: image-builder
        - podSelector:
            matchExpressions:
              - key: app
                operator: In
                values:
                  - registry-selftest-push
                  - registry-selftest-pull
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: {{ k8s_registry_port }}
    # containerd is a host process; hostPort traffic arrives SNAT'd to the cni0 gateway, which only
    # an ipBlock can admit. If kube-router skips ingress enforcement for hostPort traffic, this rule
    # is inert.
    - from:
        - ipBlock:
            cidr: {{ registry_k8s_netpol_cni_gateway }}
        # Agent pull path: host traffic entering the overlay is SNAT'd to the node's flannel.1
        # address.
        - ipBlock:
            cidr: {{ registry_k8s_netpol_agent_flannel }}
      # The ghcr.io mirror's pod has its own policy, beside its Deployment in ghcr-mirror.yaml.j2.
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: {{ k8s_registry_port }}
    # DECIDED: a standing grant for source-controller, not a hand-applied policy at break-glass
    # time. Break-glass points the OCIRepository `homelab` at this registry, so
    # source-controller must reach this port before it can fetch the artifact. A rule rendered
    # only under `flux_break_glass` would sit inside that artifact, so the operator would still
    # apply it by hand on the way in. The cost: the registry has no auth, so source-controller
    # can also push and delete here, including the `flux_job_image` copy the verify pull Jobs
    # run. That gives a compromised source-controller nothing it lacks already. It runs the
    # cosign check on every artifact itself, so it can already hand kustomize-controller an
    # unsigned bundle to apply cluster-wide.
    # namespaceSelector and podSelector are sibling keys in ONE peer, so they AND: the
    # source-controller pods in flux-system only. As two items they would OR, admitting every
    # flux-system pod and any `app: source-controller` pod in {{ k8s_namespace }}.
    - from:
        - namespaceSelector:
            matchLabels:
              kubernetes.io/metadata.name: flux-system
          podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: source-controller
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: {{ k8s_registry_port }}
